  urldate = {2026-09-22},
  abstract = {Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) are increasingly being utilized in industries such as emergency response, environmental monitoring, and precision agriculture, where obstacle avoidance and real-time path planning are crucial yet technically challenging. This research presents a framework that combines obstacle avoidance techniques, path planning algorithms, flight control, system design, and UAV classification. Representative path-planning approaches, including sampling-based, metaheuristic, machine learning, and hybrid approaches, are evaluated using key criteria such as computational cost, energy efficiency, and path optimality. The study also examines the three main steps of obstacle avoidance: obstacle perception, collision prediction, and collision avoidance. For dynamic flight missions, advanced control techniques such as adaptive learning-based controllers and model predictive control are discussed. Based on the reviewed literature, current challenges and emerging research directions are identified, such as edge computing for real-time autonomy and federated learning for knowledge sharing. The review provides a structured overview of current developments and emerging research opportunities in autonomous UAV systems.},
